================================================================================
Preamble — Purpose and Relationship to SL-META-0001
================================================================================

SL-META-0001 (the Sleela Language Metadocument) is a constitutive, conceptual
document. It establishes the design mandate, the meta-model, and the governing
rules of Sleela in prose, each rule accompanied by a rationale. It is, by its
own declaration, pre-normative and conceptual rather than formal.

This document, SL-FORMAL-0001, begins the work that SL-META-0001 repeatedly
defers to "SL-SPEC": it gives the language a formal core. Where SL-META-0001
asserts properties (for example, SEM-01 "if a program type-checks, no type
error can occur at runtime"), this document supplies the mathematical objects
that make those assertions provable rather than merely stated — a judgment
form, a core calculus, typing rules, an operational semantics, and the theorems
(Progress and Preservation) whose proof discharges SEM-01.

This document is deliberately a *core*. It formalizes a minimal fragment —
Featherweight Sleela (FS) — sufficient to pin down the load-bearing semantic
decisions (Entities, Contracts, Flows, Effects) and to expose and resolve the
internal tensions identified during review of SL-META-0001. FS is to Sleela
what Featherweight Java is to Java: not the whole language, but a faithful,
provable nucleus that the full specification (SL-SPEC) must conservatively
extend.

Status of the resolutions herein. Several sections of this document do more
than formalize existing prose: they identify contradictions within SL-META-0001
and propose resolutions. Those resolutions are collected as concrete redline
amendments in Appendix A. Until ratified by the SLDC, the amendments in
Appendix A are proposals; where this document and SL-META-0001 currently
conflict, SL-META-0001 remains authoritative and the conflict is flagged
explicitly in-line.

================================================================================
Part I — The Formal Core (Featherweight Sleela)
================================================================================

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Chapter 1 — Judgment Forms and Notation
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The reader of SL-META-0001 cannot determine what a Sleela typing judgment looks
like, because none is displayed. This chapter fixes the judgment forms used
throughout the formal core. All later rules are instances of these forms.

1.1  Metavariables

    x, y            term variables (identifiers subject to Binding)
    E               Entity names (nominal)
    C               Contract names
    o               operation names (members of a Contract)
    f              field names
    e              expressions (terms)
    v              values
    tau, sigma      types
    eps             effect rows (see Part III)
    Gamma           typing context: a finite map from variables to types
    Delta           conformance environment: sealed conformance declarations
    Sigma           store (dynamic): a finite map from locations to values

1.2  The core typing judgment

    Gamma; Delta |- e : tau ! eps

  read as: "under variable context Gamma and conformance environment Delta,
  expression e has type tau and, when evaluated, produces at most the effects
  in row eps." The effect row eps is an integral part of the judgment, not an
  afterthought: a Flow's type is meaningless without it (SL-META INV-01,
  SEM-02).

  A pure expression is one for which eps = <> (the empty row). We abbreviate

    Gamma; Delta |- e : tau        for        Gamma; Delta |- e : tau ! <>

1.3  Auxiliary judgments

    Delta |- E conforms-to C            Entity E nominally conforms to Contract C
    |- tau1 <: tau2                     tau1 is a subtype of tau2
    |- eps1 <: eps2                     effect row subsumption (Part III)
    Delta |- C sealed { E1 .. En }      C is a sealed Contract with exactly
                                        the listed conforming Entities

1.4  The evaluation judgment (dynamic semantics)

    <e, Sigma>  -->  <e', Sigma'>       small-step reduction with a store
    <e, Sigma>  -->*  <e', Sigma'>      reflexive-transitive closure

  We thread a store Sigma so that the state-mutation Effect has real dynamic
  content; a semantics without a store cannot distinguish a pure Flow from an
  effectful one and thus cannot make INV-01 meaningful.

1.5  A note on the two contexts named "context"

  SL-META-0001 uses the word "Context" for an ambient value carrier (the
  meta-model primitive). This document uses "typing context" (Gamma) in the
  standard type-theoretic sense. To avoid collision we always write "Gamma" or
  "typing context" for the former and "Context primitive" (or the ambient row
  rho of Part III) for the SL-META notion. SL-SPEC should adopt the same
  convention.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Chapter 2 — Syntax of Featherweight Sleela (FS)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

FS models the four load-bearing meta-model primitives: Entity, Contract, Flow,
Effect. Scope, Binding, Lens, Projection, Rule, and Context appear in the full
language; FS includes only what is needed to state and prove the core
metatheorems. Their formalization is scheduled for SL-SPEC and sketched where
relevant.

2.1  Grammar

  Programs
    P    ::= CD* ED* e                       contracts, entities, then a main expr

  Contract declarations
    CD   ::= contract C [sealed] { osig* }
    osig ::= o : tau -> tau ! eps            operation signature (with effects)

  Entity declarations
    ED   ::= entity E conforms C* { fd* od* }
    fd   ::= f : tau                         field declaration
    od   ::= o(x) = e                        operation definition

  Types
    tau  ::= E                               entity type (nominal)
          |  C                               contract type (structural OR sealed)
          |  Maybe tau                       optional (no null; SL-META SEM-10)
          |  tau -{eps}-> tau                flow type carrying an effect row
          |  Proj E                          projection over Entity E (read-only)

  Effect rows (detailed in Part III)
    eps  ::= <>                              pure (empty row)
          |  <l | eps>                       row extension by effect label l
          |  r                               a row variable (for inference)
    l    ::= Read | Write | Raise | IO       effect labels (extensible)

  Expressions
    e    ::= x                               variable (a Binding use)
          |  new E { f = e, .. }             entity construction
          |  e.f                             field access
          |  e.o(e)                          operation invocation
          |  flow (x : tau) ! eps => e       flow literal (declares its effects)
          |  e e                             flow application
          |  let x = e in e                  binding (final by default; SEM-08)
          |  just e  |  nothing              Maybe introduction
          |  case e of just x => e; nothing => e   exhaustive Maybe elimination
          |  raise                           performs the Raise effect
          |  handle e with { l => e }        effect discharge (Chapter 9)
          |  match e { E1 x => e; .. }       exhaustive Contract match (sealed)

  Values
    v    ::= new E { f = v, .. }
          |  flow (x : tau) ! eps => e
          |  just v  |  nothing

2.2  Well-formedness side conditions

  (WF-1) A flow literal's declared row eps must be a syntactic upper bound on
         the effects of its body (checked by rule T-FLOW, Chapter 3).
  (WF-2) `match` is only well-formed on a value whose type is a *sealed*
         Contract; on a non-sealed (structural) Contract, `match` is rejected.
         This is the syntactic half of the resolution in Part IV.
  (WF-3) `case` on Maybe must supply both arms; there is no partial unwrap.
         This is the syntactic enforcement of SEM-10.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Chapter 3 — Static Semantics (Typing Rules)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

We display the core typing rules in natural-deduction style. Premises appear
above the line, the conclusion below. These rules are the concrete content that
SL-META-0001 promised but never showed.

  (T-VAR)
        x : tau in Gamma
        --------------------------------
        Gamma; Delta |- x : tau ! <>

  (T-NEW)         entity E { f_i : tau_i } declared
                  for each i:  Gamma; Delta |- e_i : tau_i ! eps_i
        --------------------------------------------------------------
        Gamma; Delta |- new E { f_i = e_i } : E ! (join eps_i)

  (T-FIELD)       Gamma; Delta |- e : E ! eps      (f : tau) in E
        --------------------------------------------------------------
        Gamma; Delta |- e.f : tau ! eps

  (T-FLOW)        Gamma, x : tau1 ; Delta |- e : tau2 ! eps_body
                  |- eps_body <: eps            (declared row bounds the body)
        --------------------------------------------------------------
        Gamma; Delta |- (flow (x:tau1) ! eps => e) : (tau1 -{eps}-> tau2) ! <>

     A flow *literal* is itself pure (<>): constructing a flow performs no
     effects; only *applying* it does. This is the formal content of
     "Flows are first-class values" (SL-META 2.2).

  (T-APP)         Gamma; Delta |- e1 : (tau1 -{eps_f}-> tau2) ! eps_1
                  Gamma; Delta |- e2 : tau1' ! eps_2
                  |- tau1' <: tau1
        --------------------------------------------------------------
        Gamma; Delta |- e1 e2 : tau2 ! (eps_1 join eps_2 join eps_f)

     Application incurs the callee's declared effects eps_f in addition to the
     effects of evaluating the operands. This is Effect Transparency (SEM-02)
     made mechanical: the caller's row is a superset of the callee's.

  (T-LET)         Gamma; Delta |- e1 : tau1 ! eps_1
                  Gamma, x : tau1 ; Delta |- e2 : tau2 ! eps_2
        --------------------------------------------------------------
        Gamma; Delta |- let x = e1 in e2 : tau2 ! (eps_1 join eps_2)

     x is final within e2 (SEM-08). A `var` binding, omitted from FS, would add
     a Write effect on assignment.

  (T-JUST)        Gamma; Delta |- e : tau ! eps
        --------------------------------------------------------------
        Gamma; Delta |- just e : Maybe tau ! eps

  (T-NOTHING)
        --------------------------------------------------------------
        Gamma; Delta |- nothing : Maybe tau ! <>

  (T-CASE)        Gamma; Delta |- e : Maybe tau ! eps_0
                  Gamma, x : tau ; Delta |- e_j : sigma ! eps_j
                  Gamma; Delta |- e_n : sigma ! eps_n
        --------------------------------------------------------------
        Gamma; Delta |- (case e of just x => e_j; nothing => e_n)
                          : sigma ! (eps_0 join eps_j join eps_n)

     Both arms are mandatory and must agree on result type sigma. There is no
     rule that eliminates a Maybe without handling both arms: SEM-10 holds
     because no such rule exists, not because a side condition forbids it.

  (T-RAISE)
        --------------------------------------------------------------
        Gamma; Delta |- raise : tau ! <Raise>

  (T-INVOKE)      Gamma; Delta |- e : E ! eps_0
                  E conforms C in Delta     (o : tau1 -> tau2 ! eps_o) in C
                  Gamma; Delta |- e_arg : tau1' ! eps_a    |- tau1' <: tau1
        --------------------------------------------------------------
        Gamma; Delta |- e.o(e_arg) : tau2 ! (eps_0 join eps_a join eps_o)

  (T-MATCH)       Gamma; Delta |- e : C ! eps_0
                  Delta |- C sealed { E_1 .. E_n }        (must be sealed; WF-2)
                  for each k in 1..n:
                     Gamma, x_k : E_k ; Delta |- e_k : sigma ! eps_k
        --------------------------------------------------------------
        Gamma; Delta |- match e { E_k x_k => e_k } : sigma ! (join eps_0 eps_k)

     T-MATCH requires C to be sealed with a *closed* set of variants E_1..E_n,
     and requires an arm for each. Exhaustiveness is decidable precisely because
     the variant set is closed. See Part IV.

  (T-HANDLE) — see Chapter 9 (effect discharge).

  (T-SUB)         Gamma; Delta |- e : tau ! eps      |- tau <: tau'
                  |- eps <: eps'
        --------------------------------------------------------------
        Gamma; Delta |- e : tau' ! eps'

     The single subsumption rule covers both type subtyping and effect-row
     subsumption (SEM-09). Note its presence is exactly why classic
     Hindley-Milner inference does not apply unchanged (Part II).

3.1  Subtyping relation (excerpt)

  (S-REFL)  |- tau <: tau
  (S-TRANS) |- tau1 <: tau2 and |- tau2 <: tau3  =>  |- tau1 <: tau3
  (S-ENTITY) entity E2 extends E1  =>  |- E2 <: E1      (nominal, single parent)
  (S-CONTRACT-STRUCT)
             C1 declares a superset of C2's operations, each with a subsumptive
             type  =>  |- C1 <: C2          (structural; SL-META 5.4)
  (S-FLOW)   |- tau1' <: tau1   |- tau2 <: tau2'   |- eps <: eps'
             --------------------------------------------------------
             |- (tau1 -{eps}-> tau2) <: (tau1' -{eps'}-> tau2')
             (contravariant in argument, covariant in result and effects)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Chapter 4 — Dynamic Semantics (Operational Semantics)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Small-step, call-by-value, with a store Sigma so that Write/Read/Raise/IO have
observable dynamic content. Evaluation contexts capture the left-to-right,
call-by-value order that SEM-07 and SR-06 assume elsewhere.

4.1  Evaluation contexts

    Ev ::= [.]
        |  new E { f=v, .., f=Ev, e.. }
        |  Ev.f
        |  Ev e   |  v Ev
        |  Ev.o(e) | v.o(Ev)
        |  let x = Ev in e
        |  just Ev
        |  case Ev of just x => e; nothing => e
        |  match Ev { .. }
        |  handle Ev with { l => e }

4.2  Reduction rules (selected)

  (E-CTX)     <e, Sigma> --> <e', Sigma'>
              -------------------------------------
              <Ev[e], Sigma> --> <Ev[e'], Sigma'>

  (E-APP)     <(flow (x:tau)!eps => e) v, Sigma> --> <e[v/x], Sigma>

  (E-FIELD)   <(new E {.., f=v, ..}).f, Sigma> --> <v, Sigma>

  (E-LET)     <let x = v in e, Sigma> --> <e[v/x], Sigma>

  (E-CASE-J)  <case (just v) of just x => e_j; nothing => e_n, Sigma>
                 --> <e_j[v/x], Sigma>
  (E-CASE-N)  <case nothing   of just x => e_j; nothing => e_n, Sigma>
                 --> <e_n, Sigma>

  (E-MATCH)   v = new E_k {..}      arm (E_k x_k => e_k) present
              -------------------------------------------------------
              <match v { E_j x_j => e_j }, Sigma> --> <e_k[v/x_k], Sigma>

  (E-RAISE)   <Ev[raise], Sigma> --> <RAISED, Sigma>
              (propagates to the nearest enclosing `handle` for Raise; see Ch.9)

  Effectful primitives (state, IO) reduce by consulting/updating Sigma; their
  reduction is what a declared Read/Write/IO effect denotes. A flow whose type
  carries <> never triggers a store-updating reduction — this is the dynamic
  counterpart of INV-01 and is exactly the fact Preservation must maintain.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Chapter 5 — Metatheory: Progress and Preservation
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SL-META-0001 states SEM-01 ("if a Sleela program type-checks, then no type error
can occur at runtime") as a *rule*. That is backwards: type soundness is not a
rule the language obeys by fiat, it is a *theorem* about the interaction of the
typing rules (Chapter 3) and the reduction rules (Chapter 4). This chapter names
the theorems and states them precisely. Their full proofs are scheduled for
SL-SPEC; the structure below fixes exactly what must be proven.

5.1  Definition (well-typed configuration).
     A configuration <e, Sigma> is well-typed at tau ! eps if
     Gamma; Delta |- e : tau ! eps and Sigma is well-typed with respect to
     Gamma (every location holds a value of its declared type).

5.2  Theorem (Progress).
     If  {} ; Delta |- e : tau ! eps  then either
       (a) e is a value, or
       (b) e = Ev[raise] with Raise in eps and no enclosing handler (a declared,
           typed outcome — not a stuck state), or
       (c) there exist e', Sigma' with <e, Sigma> --> <e', Sigma'>.
     In particular a well-typed FS term is never *stuck* on a type error:
     it never attempts a field access on a non-entity, an application of a
     non-flow, or an unhandled Maybe unwrap.

5.3  Theorem (Preservation / Subject Reduction).
     If  Gamma; Delta |- e : tau ! eps  and  <e, Sigma> --> <e', Sigma'>
     with Sigma well-typed w.r.t. Gamma, then there exist tau', eps' with
       Gamma; Delta |- e' : tau' ! eps',
       |- tau' <: tau,  |- eps' <: eps,
     and Sigma' is well-typed w.r.t. Gamma.
     Crucially the effect row does not grow under reduction (eps' <: eps): a
     step never introduces an effect the type did not already permit. This is
     the formal content of INV-01 (Effect Declaration Completeness).

5.4  Corollary (SEM-01, discharged).
     By Progress + Preservation, a well-typed closed FS program either reduces
     to a value, or diverges, or terminates in a declared Raise outcome. It
     never reaches a type-erroneous stuck state. Thus SEM-01 holds as a derived
     property, and INV-01, INV-09, and SEM-01 are all consequences of the same
     two theorems rather than independent assertions.

5.5  Proof obligations for SL-SPEC (see Appendix C for the register):
     - Standard substitution lemma: typing is preserved under [v/x].
     - Canonical forms lemma: a value of type tau1 -{eps}-> tau2 is a flow
       literal; of type E is a `new E {..}`; of Maybe tau is just/nothing.
     - Effect weakening lemma: if Gamma;Delta |- e : tau ! eps and eps <: eps'
       then Gamma;Delta |- e : tau ! eps'.
     - Preservation for E-HANDLE must show the handled effect is *removed*
       from the row (Chapter 9), which is the only place the row shrinks.

================================================================================
Part II — Type Inference Discipline
================================================================================

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Chapter 6 — Why Not Classic Hindley-Milner
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SL-META-0001 Section 5.1 states that "the type theory basis of Sleela is
Hindley-Milner type inference, extended with (a) row polymorphism ... (b)
structural subtyping for Contracts ... (c) an effect system ..." This claim is
internally inconsistent and should be corrected.

6.1  The problem. Classic Hindley-Milner (Algorithm W) rests on the existence
     of *principal types* computed by first-order unification. Its principal-
     type property is known to fail in the presence of subtyping: once a
     subsumption rule (T-SUB, Chapter 3) is admitted, a term no longer has a
     single most-general type under unification alone — it has a most-general
     *constraint set*. SL-META-0001 admits subtyping twice: structurally for
     Contracts (5.4) and over effect rows (SEM-09). Therefore "Hindley-Milner"
     as literally stated cannot be the inference basis.

6.2  Compounding factors already present in SL-META-0001:
     - Local-only inference (5.2, "Type inference is performed locally ... not
       globally") is *not* Hindley-Milner, which is a global algorithm. This is
       actually good news (see Chapter 7) but it contradicts the HM label.
     - Mandatory annotations at export boundaries (5.2) again diverge from HM,
       which needs no annotations at all.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Chapter 7 — The Adopted Discipline: Bidirectional Checking over Algebraic Subtyping
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

We propose Sleela adopt, and SL-SPEC formalize, the following two-part
discipline. It keeps everything SL-META-0001 actually wants (local inference,
subtyping for Contracts and effects, row-typed Contexts and Effects) and drops
only the inaccurate "Hindley-Milner" label.

7.1  Bidirectional type checking at the outer level.
     Two mutually recursive judgments replace global inference:
        Gamma; Delta |- e => tau ! eps      (synthesis: e produces tau)
        Gamma; Delta |- e <= tau ! eps      (checking: e checks against tau)
     Introduction forms (flow literals, new, just/nothing) are *checked*;
     elimination forms (application, field access, invocation) *synthesize*.
     Annotations at export boundaries (SL-META 5.2) supply the checking type,
     which is exactly why the requirement exists. This makes T-SUB algorithmic:
     subsumption is applied only at the synthesis/checking mode switch.

7.2  Algebraic subtyping (MLsub / Dolan) for the inferred interior.
     Within a Flow body, where inference is desired, we adopt algebraic
     subtyping in the style of Dolan & Mycroft. It provides:
        - principal *types* even in the presence of subtyping (via polar types
          and a distinction between input and output type positions),
        - a natural, unifying treatment of *rows*, which we use for BOTH the
          Context primitive (ambient row rho) and the Effect row (eps). This
          directly realizes SL-META 5.1's "Effect row ... unified during type
          inference in a manner analogous to the Context row" — algebraic
          subtyping is the framework in which "analogous to" becomes "the same
          mechanism."
     Consequence: the two features SL-META-0001 names (row polymorphism +
     subtyping) are not bolted onto HM; they are the *native* content of
     algebraic subtyping, which subsumes HM as the subtyping-free special case.

7.3  Proposed replacement text for SL-META 5.1 — see Appendix A, Amendment A-1.

================================================================================
Part III — The Effect Algebra
================================================================================

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Chapter 8 — Effects as an Ordered Row
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SL-META-0001 describes effects two ways that do not compose: Section 5.1 calls
them a *row* (unified during inference, i.e. an open, extensible set with a tail
variable), while Section 5.4 calls them a *lattice* with `pure` at the bottom
and `impure` at the top. Row unification and lattice meet/join are different
operations over different structures. This chapter fixes a single primary
structure and derives the other as a view.

8.1  Primary structure: the effect row.
     An effect is a set of labels drawn from an extensible label universe
     {Read, Write, Raise, IO, ...}. An effect row is either closed (a finite
     label set) or open (a label set plus a row variable r for the "rest").
        eps ::= <>  |  <l | eps>  |  r
     Rows are equal up to reordering and duplicate elision: <Read | <Write>> =
     <Write | <Read>>, and <Read | <Read>> = <Read>. Formally, a closed row
     denotes a finite subset of the label universe.

8.2  Row subsumption (the ONLY subtyping on effects).
        |- eps1 <: eps2   iff   labels(eps1) is a subset of labels(eps2)
     under an appropriate treatment of the tail variable. This is SEM-09
     ("E1 <: E2 iff E1 subset of E2") and it is exactly subset ordering.

8.3  The lattice is a *derived view*, not a second structure.
     Ordered by subset inclusion, closed rows form a lattice automatically:
        bottom = <> = `pure`;   top = the full label universe = `impure`;
        join = set union;   meet = set intersection.
     SL-META 5.4's "lattice" is therefore not a competing definition but the
     poset induced by 8.2. We recommend SL-META retain the word "lattice" only
     as this derived view and define the row as primary. See Appendix A,
     Amendment A-2.

8.4  join in the typing rules. The `join` used throughout Chapter 3 (T-APP,
     T-LET, etc.) is exactly row union under 8.3. Effects accumulate by union
     as evaluation composes subterms; they never silently disappear — except at
     a handler (Chapter 9), which is the sole shrinking operation.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Chapter 9 — Effect Discharge (Handlers)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This is the single most important *addition* to the model. As written,
SL-META-0001 defines how effects are declared (SR-03), propagated (SEM-02), and
accumulated, but provides no construct that *removes* an effect from a Flow's
row. Without discharge, the effect row only ever grows: every program that does
any I/O has `IO` in the row of `main`, every subterm that touches state is
`Write`, and the effect system degrades from a type-and-effect system into a
monotone taint tracker. Nothing can ever be made pure again by handling it.

9.1  The handle construct.
        handle e with { l => h }
     evaluates e; if e performs effect l, control transfers to handler h. The
     handled label l is *removed* from the resulting row.

  (T-HANDLE)   Gamma; Delta |- e : tau ! eps
               Gamma; Delta |- h : tau ! eps_h
               ------------------------------------------------------------
               Gamma; Delta |- handle e with { l => h }
                                : tau ! ((eps minus l) join eps_h)

     The result row is (eps with l removed) unioned with whatever the handler
     itself does. If the handler is pure and l was the only effect, the whole
     expression becomes pure again. This is the ONLY typing rule in which the
     effect row shrinks, and it is what makes Preservation's "eps' <: eps"
     (5.3) hold everywhere else.

  (E-HANDLE-VAL)  <handle v with { l => h }, Sigma> --> <v, Sigma>
  (E-HANDLE-RAISE) for l = Raise:
        <handle Ev[raise] with { Raise => h }, Sigma> --> <h, Sigma>
        (nearest enclosing Raise-handler catches; models exception discharge)

9.2  Consequence for the Read/Write/IO labels. A `handle e with { IO => ... }`
     that supplies IO via an in-memory implementation discharges the IO label,
     enabling pure testing of effectful Flows — a concrete engineering payoff
     that motivates the whole effect system (SL-META 1.1). Without Chapter 9
     this payoff is unreachable.

9.3  Proposed new rule for SL-META Chapter 5 — Appendix A, Amendment A-3
     (SEM-11 Effect Discharge Rule) and a companion runtime rule.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Chapter 10 — Correcting the Read/Write Lattice Direction
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SL-META-0001 Section 5.4 states: "Read Effects are subtypes of Write Effects."
Combined with SEM-09 ("a Flow promising fewer Effects is substitutable for one
promising more") this produces the wrong substitutability.

10.1 The bug. Under 8.2, "Read <: Write" would have to mean labels({Read}) is a
     subset of labels({Write}), which is false — they are distinct labels, so
     neither is a subset of the other. If instead 5.4 intends an *ordering
     among labels themselves* (a capability order where Write implies Read),
     then the correct reading is: a flow that only Reads performs *fewer*
     effects than one that Writes, so

        <Read>  <:  <Read, Write>       (reader substitutable for writer-context)

     i.e. {Read} is the subset. The direction "Read is a subtype of Write"
     read as flows is inverted: a Read-only flow is the SAFE substitute where a
     more-capable flow is expected, consistent with SEM-09, precisely because
     its label set is smaller.

10.2 Resolution. Do not order the labels Read and Write against each other at
     all. Treat Write as the pair {Read, Write} at the point of use if the
     intent is "writing implies the capability to read"; then subset ordering
     (8.2) gives the correct and only substitutability, with no special-case
     lattice edge. See Appendix A, Amendment A-4, which deletes the sentence
     "Read Effects are subtypes of Write Effects" and replaces it with the
     subset treatment.

================================================================================
Part IV — Conformance, Sealing, and Exhaustiveness
================================================================================

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Chapter 11 — The Structural/Sealed Contradiction and Its Resolution
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Three provisions of SL-META-0001 are individually reasonable but jointly
inconsistent:

  (i)   5.2 / 5.4: Contract conformance is STRUCTURAL — "an Entity conforms to a
        Contract if and only if it structurally implements all of the Contract's
        required operations, regardless of whether it explicitly declares
        conformance."
  (ii)  SR-04 + "Sealed Hierarchies by Default" (5.2): a Pattern Block matching
        a Contract-typed value must be EXHAUSTIVE over "every Entity known to
        conform to that Contract within the compilation unit."
  (iii) INV-03: `abstract contract` permits conformance from OTHER modules.

11.1 Why they collide. If conformance is structural, then "the set of Entities
     that conform to C" is not a declared, closed set — it is every present or
     future Entity, in this or any other module, that happens to have the right
     shape. Exhaustiveness checking (ii) requires a CLOSED set of variants.
     Structural conformance makes the set OPEN. Therefore exhaustiveness over a
     structurally-conformed Contract is unsound: a new conforming Entity added
     in another module (permitted by iii) silently invalidates every existing
     `match`, with no error at the match site. This breaks the safety SR-04
     claims to provide.

11.2 Resolution — split the concept into two kinds of Contract.

     SEALED (nominal) Contracts:
        - Conformance is by EXPLICIT declaration only (`entity E conforms C`).
        - The set of conforming Entities is closed and known at the declaring
          module. New conformers outside the module are rejected unless C is
          declared `open`.
        - `match` / Pattern Blocks are permitted and exhaustiveness is checked
          against the closed variant set. This is FS's T-MATCH (Chapter 3),
          which requires `Delta |- C sealed { E_1..E_n }`.

     STRUCTURAL (open) Contracts:
        - Conformance is structural, as in current 5.2 — any Entity of the
          right shape conforms, in any module.
        - `match` / Pattern Blocks are NOT permitted on a structural Contract
          (WF-2 in Chapter 2). Consumers use operation invocation (duck-typed
          dispatch), never exhaustive casing.

     The distinction is exactly the one Scala (sealed trait vs. trait), Kotlin
     (sealed interface vs. interface), and Rust (enum vs. trait objects) draw,
     and for the same reason. It preserves both goals of SL-META-0001 — the
     flexibility of structural conformance AND the safety of exhaustive
     matching — by never applying them to the same Contract.

11.3 Effect on SR-04, SEM-05, and 5.2. SR-04 should say "a Pattern Block may
     match only on a SEALED Contract, and must be exhaustive over that
     Contract's declared conforming Entities." SEM-05 (Contract Conformance)
     remains correct for both kinds; only the *matchability* differs. See
     Appendix A, Amendment A-5.

================================================================================
Part V — Lens Laws
================================================================================

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Chapter 12 — The Three Lens Laws and the Effectful-Lens Restriction
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SL-META-0001 states the lens laws in two places that disagree, and both are
incomplete:
  - INV-04 lists only  s(a, g(a)) = a          (GetPut)
  - SEM-04 lists  g(s(a,v)) = v  (PutGet)  and  s(a, g(a)) = a  (GetPut)
Neither states PutPut, which is required for the "Lenses ... form a category"
and "composition is associative" claims of SL-META 2.3.

12.1 The three laws. A well-behaved Lens L with get g : A -> B and
     set s : A x B -> A must satisfy:

        (GetPut)  s(a, g(a)) = a
                    "setting the value you just got changes nothing"
        (PutGet)  g(s(a, v)) = v
                    "getting after setting returns what you set"
        (PutPut)  s(s(a, v1), v2) = s(a, v2)
                    "a later set overrides an earlier one"

     A Lens satisfying GetPut + PutGet is *well-behaved*. A Lens ALSO satisfying
     PutPut is *very well-behaved*. Only very-well-behaved lenses compose into a
     lawful category with associative composition; therefore SL-META 2.3's
     categorical claim REQUIRES PutPut. Both INV-04 and SEM-04 must be amended
     to state all three. See Appendix A, Amendment A-6.

12.2 Effectful lenses (SR-09) and the laws. SR-09 permits a Lens whose `set`
     declares effects ("effectful lens"). But the three laws above are
     EQUATIONAL and presuppose that get and set are pure functions; the equation
     g(s(a,v)) = v is not even well-formed if s performs I/O, because s(a,v) is
     then not a value but an effectful computation. Resolution:

        - The algebraic laws (12.1) and the "forms a category" guarantee apply
          ONLY to pure lenses.
        - An effectful lens is a DIFFERENT object: a pair of Flows with a
          non-empty effect row. It does not claim the lens laws and MUST NOT be
          composed via the categorical `>>` operator (SL-META 2.3), which is
          defined only for lawful lenses.
        - SR-09's allowance stands, but SL-META must state that effectful lenses
          forfeit the algebraic guarantees and the composition algebra. See
          Appendix A, Amendment A-7.

================================================================================
Part VI — Determinism
================================================================================

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Chapter 13 — Canonical Ordering for Deterministic Compilation
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CR-03 (Deterministic IR) requires byte-identical IR "regardless of platform,
thread scheduling, or hash seed randomization." This cannot be guaranteed by
decree: several compiler operations iterate over sets, and set iteration order
is exactly what hash-seed randomization perturbs. Determinism must be *designed
in* by fixing a total order at every such site.

13.1 Sites that iterate over unordered collections:
     - Structural conformance discovery (which Entities conform to a structural
       Contract) — a set membership computation.
     - Rule evaluation across compilation units — SL-META 2.3 says
       "topological dependency order," but topological order is not unique.
     - Effect-row and Context-row canonicalization — rows are equal up to
       reordering (8.1), so a canonical serialization order is needed.
     - Contract operation sets, Entity field sets, Derive Block output.

13.2 The canonical order rule (proposed). At every site above, iteration and
     emission proceed in a single total order defined as:
        primary key:   source declaration order within a compilation unit;
        cross-unit:    a topological sort broken by fully-qualified name
                        (lexicographic, NFC-normalized UTF-8 code points);
        rows/sets with no source order (e.g. inferred effect rows):
                        sorted by label name, then by origin position.
     This makes "topological dependency order" (2.3) and CR-03 realizable:
     the tie-break by FQN removes the freedom that non-unique topological sorts
     and hashed set iteration would otherwise leave. See Appendix A,
     Amendment A-8, which adds this as CR-09 (Canonical Ordering Rule).

================================================================================
Appendix A — Redline: Specific Amendments Proposed to SL-META-0001
================================================================================

Each amendment is a concrete, ratifiable change. Format: [target] OLD -> NEW.

A-1  [SL-META 5.1, type theory basis]
     OLD: "The type theory basis of Sleela is Hindley-Milner type inference,
           extended with (a) row polymorphism ... (b) structural subtyping ...
           (c) an effect system ..."
     NEW: "The type discipline of Sleela is bidirectional type checking at
           declaration boundaries over an algebraic-subtyping core (in the style
           of Dolan & Mycroft's MLsub). Algebraic subtyping natively provides
           principal types in the presence of subtyping, and a single row
           mechanism that serves both the Context primitive and the Effect
           system. Classic Hindley-Milner is the subtyping-free special case.
           Full inference is confined to Flow bodies; module-exported
           declarations require explicit annotations, which supply the checking
           direction." (See SL-FORMAL-0001 Chapters 6-7.)

A-2  [SL-META 5.4, effect structure]
     Reconcile with SL-META 5.1: declare the EFFECT ROW primary and the LATTICE
     derived. Add: "Effects form an extensible row; row subsumption is subset
     inclusion (SEM-09). The effect lattice of this section is the poset induced
     by that subset order: bottom = pure = <>, top = impure = full label set,
     join = union, meet = intersection. It is a derived view, not an independent
     structure." (See SL-FORMAL-0001 Chapter 8.)

A-3  [SL-META Chapter 5, NEW rule SEM-11; and Chapter 7, NEW runtime rule]
     ADD SEM-11 Effect Discharge Rule: "A `handle` construct discharges a named
     effect label from the effect row of its guarded expression. The handled
     label is removed from the resulting Flow's declared Effect set; this is the
     only operation that reduces an Effect row. A Flow all of whose effects are
     discharged becomes pure." (See SL-FORMAL-0001 Chapter 9.)
     Rationale: without discharge the Effect system is a monotone taint tracker,
     not a type-and-effect system, and effectful Flows can never be tested
     purely.

A-4  [SL-META 5.4, Effect Subtyping paragraph]
     OLD: "Read Effects are subtypes of Write Effects."
     NEW: (delete the sentence). "Effect labels are not ordered against one
           another; the only subtyping on effects is subset inclusion of label
           sets (SEM-09). Where a capability model is intended (writing implies
           the ability to read), model Write at a use site as the set
           {Read, Write}; subset inclusion then yields the correct and only
           substitutability, with a Read-only Flow substitutable where a
           more-capable Flow is expected." (See SL-FORMAL-0001 Chapter 10.)

A-5  [SL-META 5.2 / 5.4 / SR-04, conformance and matching]
     ADD the sealed/structural Contract distinction:
       - Sealed (nominal) Contracts: conformance by explicit declaration;
         closed variant set; Pattern Blocks permitted with exhaustiveness
         checked against the declared conformers.
       - Structural (open) Contracts: structural conformance as in current 5.2;
         Pattern Blocks NOT permitted; consumers use operation invocation only.
     AMEND SR-04: "A Pattern Block may match only on a SEALED Contract and must
       be exhaustive over that Contract's declared conforming Entities (or
       provide an explicit wildcard)." (See SL-FORMAL-0001 Chapter 11.)

A-6  [SL-META INV-04 and SEM-04, lens laws]
     REPLACE both with the full set of three laws:
       (GetPut)  s(a, g(a)) = a
       (PutGet)  g(s(a, v)) = v
       (PutPut)  s(s(a, v1), v2) = s(a, v2)
     State that PutPut is required for the categorical composition claim of
     SL-META 2.3, and that INV-04 and SEM-04 must agree. (See SL-FORMAL-0001
     Chapter 12.)

A-7  [SL-META SR-09, effectful lenses]
     ADD: "The Lens laws (INV-04/SEM-04) and the Lens composition algebra
     (2.3) apply only to pure Lenses. An effectful Lens forfeits the algebraic
     guarantees and may not be composed via the categorical composition
     operator." (See SL-FORMAL-0001 Chapter 12.2.)

A-8  [SL-META Chapter 6, NEW rule CR-09]
     ADD CR-09 Canonical Ordering Rule: "Wherever the compiler iterates over or
     serializes an unordered collection (structural conformance sets, cross-unit
     rule order, effect/Context rows, field and operation sets), it must use a
     single total order: source declaration order within a unit; cross-unit
     topological order broken by NFC-normalized fully-qualified name; and for
     collections with no source order, sorted by name then origin. This makes
     CR-03 (Deterministic IR) realizable." (See SL-FORMAL-0001 Chapter 13.)

A-9  [SL-META cross-cutting, rule/invariant direction]
     Adopt one direction consistently: INVARIANTS are properties that hold of
     well-formed programs; RULES are the mechanisms (typing/compilation) that
     enforce them. Where a rule restates an invariant (SR-03 vs INV-01,
     SR-07 vs INV-07, SEM-06 vs INV-05), the Rule Index (Appendix C of SL-META)
     should cross-link "rule R enforces invariant I" rather than duplicating the
     statement. (Editorial; no semantic change.)

================================================================================
Appendix B — Open Questions for the SLDC
================================================================================

B-1  Context suppression vs. monotonic propagation. SEM-03 forbids removing keys
     from an ambient Context downstream, but 2.3 permits a "context barrier" that
     suppresses a Context. Is suppression (a) shadowing (key present but
     inaccessible — compatible with SEM-03), or (b) removal (row minus key —
     violates SEM-03)? This document assumes (a). SLDC to confirm.

B-2  Rules as predicates vs. rewriters. 2.3 / INV-08 treat Rules both as
     constraints (predicates over the AST) and as transformations (which can
     rewrite the AST and thereby interfere, INV-08). These need different
     soundness arguments. Should FS/SL-SPEC restrict compile-time Rules to pure
     predicates, with rewriting confined to Desugaring (Phase 3)?

B-3  Diagnostic namespace. SL-E-XXXX / SL-W-0001 are referenced but never
     defined. Propose a stability policy: codes are permanent, never reused or
     renumbered; a registry file governs allocation.

B-4  Version taxonomy. Three version axes exist (syntax pragma `#sleela M.N`,
     document revision, SRL version). A single normative table should state
     which governs what and the permitted drift between them.

B-5  Non-goal framing. "Not a research language" (1.3) sits uneasily beside a
     language-level effect system, row polymorphism, and algebraic lenses.
     Recommend a sentence acknowledging these are ambitious features adopted for
     engineering payoff, not theoretical novelty — so the non-goal is not read
     as inaccurate.

